WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:03.545
Hey, guys, we are so happy
that you came back

00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:05.964
for another episode
of the Wine Down.

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.341
Today, we are joined
by Jean Elie,

00:00:08.425 --> 00:00:10.552
who plays Issa's brother,
Ahmal.

00:00:10.802 --> 00:00:13.012
To talk about
"Lowkey Movin' On."

00:00:13.096 --> 00:00:15.140
♪ (UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:18.101
You know, you and Molly
have just had sort of, like--

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:19.728
You know like in season one,
you guys

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:22.188
have argumentative fights,
but this fight gets--

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:24.107
You guys physically step
to each other.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.693
So, do you think Issa
and Molly can, sort of,

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:27.777
come back from this?

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:30.405
Well, I would say
guys are a unique breed.

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:31.448
Yeah, we're completely
different.

00:00:31.781 --> 00:00:33.116
It's like y'all fight
and you become best friends.

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.033
Right.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:35.744
Once women fight, it's a wrap.

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:37.996
-It was a public violation too.
-Yeah.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.748
There's an embarrassment
element to it.

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:41.541
It was at Issa's event.
So...

00:00:41.624 --> 00:00:44.711
I don't know how you could
come back from that

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:48.131
without having a fully-candid
conversation.

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.467
Like I-- For me,
I could never look

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.719
-at my friend the same way.
-It's like--

00:00:52.802 --> 00:00:55.680
It's like, uh, a mirror broke,
and then now you put it

00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:57.557
back together, and you can still
see cracks in it.

00:00:57.766 --> 00:00:59.142
Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.769
So, wait, how do you come back
from that?

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:02.395
-(SIGHS)
-How do you come back

00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:03.730
from a cracky-ass mirror?

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:04.856
-Just be looking at--
-Just remember--

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.901
Just look at your little--
little section, right here,

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:08.777
and you good.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:09.986
You get used
to that reflection

00:01:10.069 --> 00:01:10.987
after a while?

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.406
Like... (WINCES) I get it.
(CHUCKLES)

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:16.618
One of my favorite moments
this episode

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.912
is Kelli pretending
to be British.

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:21.790
And you jumping
to call her out on it.

00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:22.999
-(CHUCKLES)
-Can you talk about,

00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:26.628
just in you filming that scene,
and the Ahmal and Kelli dynamic

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:28.505
-is... great.
-JEAN ELIE: I think Kelli

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:33.384
is brilliantly basic.
She's so brilliant, she's basic.

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:36.137
And the fact that she thought
she was gonna pass off

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:39.390
South Central London.
Like... (CHUCKLES)

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:42.560
Come on, are you serious?
(CHUCKLES)

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:45.396
So-- And I just loved
how Ahmal just literally

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.774
pokes holes in her story
the whole time.

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:49.984
And she's literally trying
to cover up...

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:51.653
every time. (CHUCKLES)

00:01:51.736 --> 00:01:53.780
So one of the things
I think we can pose

00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:56.741
is I wanted to talk about
Issa's impostor syndrome

00:01:56.825 --> 00:01:59.118
at the beginning of the episode.
And then, finally,

00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:00.912
giving herself credit
for pulling it off

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:02.539
-at the block party.
-Yeah.

00:02:02.872 --> 00:02:06.459
That's real.
I mean we've all had projects,

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:09.254
we've all, you know,
put ourselves out there.

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:12.757
And there's no worse feeling
than feeling like...

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:15.635
"Why did I think that
I could actually pull this off?"

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:16.553
-Yeah.
-Like--

00:02:16.886 --> 00:02:19.305
And-- But she did it.
Like, no one can take that

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:20.640
-from her.
-Yeah, she sure did.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.600
PRENTICE PENNY: You know
that energy is good, right?

00:02:22.684 --> 00:02:25.144
That energy of like...
"I don't want to be seen

00:02:25.228 --> 00:02:27.105
as an impostor--" And obviously,
Issa, the character,

00:02:27.188 --> 00:02:30.441
has had a lot of big ideas.
And not always

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:32.694
the best follow-through.
And I think this is like,

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:35.405
"Oh, I've got investors,
and people putting their money

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.491
in on this thing.
And this is like, I think,

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:39.909
for my professional career
as well..."

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.119
This ain't like something
that's just like,

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.704
"Oh, it's just gonna get seen
by my girlfriends."

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:44.497
This is like, "Oh, I did this
for the neighborhood."

00:02:44.581 --> 00:02:45.415
And there is a--

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:47.250
And I think,
even in the back of her mind,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:51.254
like her best friend
didn't want to ask her boyfriend

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:53.840
because she didn't necessarily
fully believe

00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:56.050
that this wasn't one of those
schemes, you know?

00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:57.969
That it wasn't going
to be executed properly.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:02:59.095
That it could actually
pan out.

00:02:59.429 --> 00:03:03.057
Exactly, so there's an element
of, like, proving herself.

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:07.270
And that pressure is
the fucking worst.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.106
How have y'all dealt
with impostor syndrome?

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.400
When you know you're capable?

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:15.153
Ooh, I'm still
working through that right now.

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:17.196
Like, I literally Googled
impostor syndrome,

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.032
like, "Oh, I deal with this shit
all the time."

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:21.409
And I'm like--
I'm trying to work through it.

00:03:21.492 --> 00:03:23.870
Really just calling myself
on my shit, you know?

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:26.789
Um... journal.
And then, um,

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.791
the people around me
literally help.

00:03:29.083 --> 00:03:30.960
Yeah.
For me, the fear is...

00:03:31.586 --> 00:03:34.797
Not necessarily that I'm going
to be found out,

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.925
as when I'm projecting something
I'm not...

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:39.844
But that who I am isn't enough.
And that--

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:41.804
-That is--
-That's exactly what it is.

00:03:42.013 --> 00:03:43.389
I remember one time
I was on a show,

00:03:43.473 --> 00:03:44.849
and one of the best things
I thought the showrunner

00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:46.309
ever did was say,
"I don't know."

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:48.895
And it was so freeing,
that I just was like,

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:51.522
"Oh, that's a very easy--"
Like, nobody thinks of you

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:53.733
any less.
I think that's the fear,

00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:55.151
is that we won't be enough.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.236
I'ma use that.
But I don't know.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.112
ISSA RAE: Yeah.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.365
Well, as always,
thank you for Wining Down

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.783
with us.
And be sure to come back

00:04:01.866 --> 00:04:03.785
next week after we break down
episode six.

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:05.620
-Um, see you guys then.
-(CHEERING)

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:06.579
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

00:04:06.663 --> 00:04:07.789
Halfway through,
halfway through.

00:04:07.872 --> 00:04:08.957
Yeah, halfway through.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.500
♪ (MUSIC CONCLUDES) ♪

